{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["The case of the betrothed girl (נערה המאורסה): when witnesses see from a distance a man seizing a girl and lying with her in the city, and the witnesses raise their voice and warn them, according to our Rabbis (Makkot 6b) both are liable to stoning. For the woman too has the presumption of being a willing adulteress, since she did not cry out at all, for the way of any raped woman (אנוסה) is to cry out in the city to be rescued and saved. But if they see her in the field and he seizes her and lies with her, she has the presumption of being raped (אנוסה) and is exempt. And the meaning of <b>she cried out</b> (צעקה) — it is possible that she did cry out. Or its meaning is: if she cried out and there is no rescuer for her — for even if they heard that she did not cry out, she is exempt since there are no rescuers for her there. And the general principle: if she has rescuers, whether in the city or in the field, she is liable; if she has no rescuers, whether in the city or in the field, she is exempt — for Scripture spoke only of the typical case (הווה)." "And Scripture says <b>because he afflicted the wife of his fellow</b> (על דבר אשר ענה את אשת רעהו) — and the term עינוי (afflicting) is not applied only to one who lies with a woman by force, and this woman has already been held liable as a willing adulteress. Rather, the meaning of the verse is that when it is seen that the man seized her and lay with her, the woman is judged as willing, because she could have been rescued from him, and the man is considered as one who afflicted her (מענה אותה), for he did not seduce her nor speak to her heart to submit to him. And I do not know regarding the law of crying out: if we saw a girl whom the man seized and she fights him with all her strength and weeps and clutches his garments or his hair to escape from him, yet did not know to cry out — why should she be stoned? Rather, the matter of crying out too is part of the typical case: she is judged by the general rule — in the city she is presumed seduced (מפותה), and in the field raped (אנוסה)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The sentence 'it is possible that she cried out' (יתכן שצעקה) addresses whether the verse's language is descriptive of what actually happened; Ramban proceeds to offer a second reading.", 1 "The phrase 'I do not know' introduces a difficulty: a woman physically resisting but not screaming does not fit the simple binary of cried/did not cry out; Ramban resolves by noting the verse's categories describe the typical case, not every conceivable scenario."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:53:47.785487508Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}